<%# The page the emailed link opens. Renders for BOTH states — a live link and
    a spent or expired one — because "this link has already been used" is the
    single most likely reason someone lands here and deserves a sentence, not a
    404. Nothing is consumed by loading this page; the button below is what
    signs anyone in. %>
<div class="you-page">
  <div class="you-orbs" aria-hidden="true"><i class="a"></i><i class="b"></i><i class="c"></i><i class="d"></i></div>
  <div class="you-shell you-shell-narrow">
    <div class="you-mark"><%= image_tag "playverto.svg", alt: "Playverto", width: 120 %></div>

    <% if @link %>
      <%# Two headings, because this page now serves two arrivals. A link out of
          an inbox is being confirmed by someone who went and fetched it; a
          signup link was handed straight back a moment ago on the end screen,
          and telling that person "this link was sent to you" describes an email
          that was never sent. %>
      <div class="you-card">
        <h1 class="you-h1">
          <%= t(@link.proves_address? ? "player_sign_in.title" : "player_sign_in.signup_title") %>
        </h1>
        <p class="you-sub">
          <%= @link.proves_address? ? t("player_sign_in.body", email: @link.player.email_address)
                                    : t("player_sign_in.signup_body") %>
        </p>
        <%= button_to t("player_sign_in.cta"), player_sign_in_path(params[:token]),
              method: :post, class: "you-btn you-btn-primary",
              form: { id: "player-sign-in-continue", style: "margin:0;" } %>
        <%# A signup link was handed straight back to THIS browser a moment ago
            on the end screen, so there is nobody to confirm anything to — and
            the tap was costing real accounts. On 15 September, of the people
            who typed an address and a password: 15 of 39 in the room never
            pressed it, against 1 of 14 away from one. They all have an account
            and a password; they just never landed in it.

            The POST still does the work and is not being bypassed. It is the
            only request in this flow with a usable cookie jar: #join runs
            under protect_from_forgery :null_session, because the play page is
            cached by the service worker and its CSRF token can be arbitrarily
            stale, and under null_session a cookie write is a no-op. That is
            why a link exists at all rather than #join simply signing someone
            in. This only spares a person pressing a button on their own behalf.

            Inline on purpose, and not a Stimulus controller: controllers are
            lazy-loaded (config/importmap.rb pins them preload: false), so one
            more module would have to arrive before this could fire — and the
            people it is for were on congested venue wifi, which is exactly
            where a module fetch is least likely to land. This runs at parse
            time with no dependency on the bundle. The button above is the
            no-JS path and still works untouched. %>
        <% if @auto_continue %>
          <script>
            (function () {
              var f = document.getElementById("player-sign-in-continue");
              if (f && f.requestSubmit) f.requestSubmit();
            })();
          </script>
        <% end %>
        <p class="you-fine"><%= t("player_sign_in.fine", minutes: (PlayerSignInLink::LIFETIME / 60).to_i) %></p>
      </div>
    <% else %>
      <div class="you-card">
        <h1 class="you-h1"><%= t("player_sign_in.spent_title") %></h1>
        <p class="you-sub"><%= t("player_sign_in.spent_body") %></p>
        <%= link_to t("player_sign_in.spent_cta"), you_path, class: "you-btn" %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
